{
  "dom_id": "step-q181139",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "The solution to the inequality $-2u>-1$ is $u${{field-1}}{{field-2}}.",
  "worked_solution": "The variable $u$ is being *multiplied* by $-2.$ To isolate $u,$ we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-2.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2u\\,\\, & >\\,\\,-1 \\\\ \\frac{-2u}{-2}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{-1}{-2} \\\\ \\frac{-2u}{-2}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-2$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-2u}{-2}\\,\\, & <\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-2}u}{\\cancel{-2}} & <\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ u & <\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $u<\\frac{1}{2}.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
